Research Note 2: Residential Real Estate is Not Synonymous with AVM Readiness

Title: Residential Property Markets Differ in AVM Readiness

Motivation

Much AVM literature implicitly assumes that residential real estate represents a homogeneous environment suitable for automated valuation. This assumption does not hold across jurisdictions.

Core Proposition

> Residential property markets differ substantially in their readiness for AVM deployment according to market observability and valuation data suitability.



Residential property should therefore not be treated as a single modelling environment.

Proposed Taxonomy

Market	Transaction Activity	Data Suitability	AVM Maturity

US Residential	High	High	Mature
Indian Tier-1 Residential	High	Moderate	Emerging
Indian Tier-2/3 Residential	Moderate	Low	Limited
Commercial Real Estate	Low–Moderate	Variable	Model-Assisted


Implications

Residential property is not automatically AVM-ready.

Commercial real estate is not necessarily outside quantitative valuation, but generally depends on model-assisted approaches and expert judgement.

The distinguishing characteristic is market observability and valuation data suitability, not merely the asset class.


Research Contribution

This shifts the discussion from:

> Residential versus Commercial



to

> High-observability versus Low-observability valuation environments.



Valuation governance then becomes a function of uncertainty arising from imperfect valuation evidence, irrespective of whether the collateral is residential or commercial.


